  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  A few gusty thunderstorms possible across \nportions of the Lower and Middle Susquehanna Valley this \nafternoon and evening.\n\nA weak upper level disturbance embedded in the NW flow aloft\nwill race SE and over a favorable zone of convergence along a\nlee trough that will be situated across the Middle and Lower\nSusq Valley this afternoon/early evening. \n\nSurface dewpoints in the low to mid 60s convergence and the\npassage of the left exit region of a 90 kt jet max over Ohio and\nSW PA late this afternoon will provide the fuel and favorable\nlarger scale UVVEL for some pulse and small clusters of \nstrong to potentially svr multicell TSRA across the I-81 and RT\n11/15 corridors in southern PA within a fairly narrow corridor\nof sfc-based CAPE between 1000-1500 J/KG between 18Z-00Z. \nThough dry air aloft and weak shear should help to limit updraft\nstrength, a few stronger storms may produce isolated strong to \nsevere wind gusts this afternoon. SPC has maintained the \nmarginal risk for severe weather across the Susquehanna Valley.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Turning more humid and unsettled early in the\nupcoming week with the highest probability for showers and \nthunderstorms Monday afternoon and evening and again on Tuesday \nand Tuesday night.\n\nThere is still some uncertainty regarding the coverage of \nstorms during the day on Monday as upper level forcing is \nexpected to remain weak. We should see at least scattered \nthunderstorm development during the afternoon as modest \ninstability develops, but shear will remain weak. The severe \nthreat appears to be low, but a few strong to severe wind gusts \nwill be possible with any stronger storms. Some models show an \nMCS diving south from the Great Lakes Monday night which could \nimpact western portions of the area, but confidence is low on \ntiming and placement of this feature.\n\nTuesday will likely see the most widespread showers and\nthunderstorms of the week as a potent shortwave approaches from\nthe west and its associated surface low tracks over the region.\nShowers and storms will likely be ongoing across parts of\nCentral PA Tuesday morning and should continue through the rest\nof the day. The potential for severe weather remains uncertain\nas it will depend on much we are able to destabilize behind the\nmorning showers and storms. SPC has areas roughly along and \nsouth of I-80 in a marginal risk for severe weather and they\nhave included far southern portions of the area in a slight\nrisk.\n\nHeavy rain will also be a concern Monday evening into Tuesday\nwith any storms that develop as PWAT values will climb to around\n1.8 inches. Storms will be very efficient rainfall producers and\nareas that see repeated rounds of thunderstorms may see some\nflash flooding concerns develop. WPC has placed nearly all of\nCentral PA in a marginal risk for excessive rainfall for both\nMonday and Tuesday.",
